{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_yvmxw5xlsbi5xf5rxmn3pswdrgnkpouqpefbtnkn7ystyyhuybya","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01YRtTMJUp9464V24CMN9A53","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Enumerate compile error triggers","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":false,"prompt":"Working dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wwiWoN . READ ONLY on c_src/. Do NOT modify c_src/, do NOT modify translation/src/, do NOT create SYMBOLS.md/ERRORS.md/CONFIGS.md.\n\nTASK: produce a data file `_v/err_compile.tsv` listing, for EVERY compile-time error code ERR0..ERR120 of PCRE2 (defined in c_src/src/pcre2_compile.h line 53-65 as `enum { ERR0 = COMPILE_ERROR_BASE, ERR1, ... ERR120 }`, COMPILE_ERROR_BASE=100, so ERRn == numeric error code 100+n), the exact rejection condition and a CONCRETE pattern that triggers it.\n\nHow to find them: the C sources set them via things like `*errorcodeptr = ERRnn;`, `errorcode = ERRnn;`, `cb->erroroffset = ...`, and `return ERRnn`. Grep with:\n  grep -nE \"ERR[0-9]+\" c_src/src/pcre2_compile.c c_src/src/pcre2_compile_class.c c_src/src/pcre2_compile_cgroup.c c_src/src/pcre2_study.c c_src/src/pcre2_auto_possess.c c_src/src/pcre2_convert.c c_src/src/pcre2_error.c\nThe human-readable message for each code is in the `compile_error_texts` string table in c_src/src/pcre2_error.c (they are in ERR1, ERR2, ... order, NUL-separated) — use it to know what each code means.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT: tab-separated, one line per ERR code, exactly 7 fields, no header:\n  <errname>\\t<numeric_code>\\t<c_file:line>\\t<short trigger description>\\t<pattern>\\t<compile_options>\\t<extra_options>\nwhere\n * <pattern> is a Rust byte-string literal body (what goes inside b\"...\") that triggers this error, using \\x## escapes for non-ASCII/quote/backslash chars. Remember Rust escaping: a regex backslash is written \\\\ .\n * <compile_options> is a Rust expression using the PCRE2_* constant names (e.g. `0`, `PCRE2_UTF`, `PCRE2_UTF|PCRE2_CASELESS`, `PCRE2_NEVER_UTF`, `PCRE2_ALT_EXTENDED_CLASS`, `PCRE2_LITERAL|PCRE2_MULTILINE`...).\n * <extra_options> is a Rust expression of PCRE2_EXTRA_* constants set via pcre2_set_compile_extra_options (or `0`).\n * If a code is unreachable in the 8-bit / SUPPORT_UNICODE / no-JIT / LINK_SIZE=2 build (e.g. errors only for 16/32-bit, EBCDIC, or \"internal error\" codes that need a corrupted state), put the pattern field as `UNREACHABLE` and describe why in the description field.\n\nBe accurate: derive each trigger from reading the actual C code around the assignment. You MUST cover all 121 codes ERR0..ERR120 (one line each, in numeric order).\n\nVERIFY YOUR WORK before finishing: write a small C program that #includes <pcre2.h> (`-I c_src/include -DPCRE2_CODE_UNIT_WIDTH=8`), links c_src/build/libpcre2.so, and for each row compiles the pattern with the given options/extra-options and prints the actual error code. Compare to the expected code and FIX the pattern rows that do not produce the expected error. Iterate until every reachable row produces exactly its expected error code. Put that C program in _v/checkerr.c and leave it there.\n\nFINAL REPORT: state how many rows total, how many verified-correct by running the C program, how many UNREACHABLE, and list the ERR codes you could not trigger."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":510,"cache_read_input_tokens":83891,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":510,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4f10895-a33e-435f-9410-97f386d0c503","uuid":"0f3cbe9c-2941-45df-a45b-c4ec9f160d1f","timestamp":"2026-09-08T17:27:15.408Z"}
