tions in pcre2_context.c return 0 or PCRE2_ERROR_BADDATA for out-of-range values (e.g. pcre2_set_newline with an invalid convention, pcre2_set_bsr, pcre2_set_glob_separator/escape, pcre2_set_optimize with an unknown directive) — cover every one of those checks.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT: tab-separated, 6 fields, one row per DISTINCT rejection, no header:\n  <public function name, e.g. pcre2_match_8>\\t<expected result: exact constant name and numeric value, e.g. PCRE2_ERROR_BADOFFSET(-33) or NULL or FALSE(0)>\\t<c_file:line>\\t<trigger condition exactly as the C tests it>\\t<RECIPE>\\t<notes>\nThe RECIPE field must be a precise, self-contained description of the API call sequence needed to reach it, e.g.\n  \"compile b\\\"a\\\" opts 0; match_data_create(1); pcre2_match(code, subj=b\\\"abc\\\", len=3, startoffset=4, 0, md, NULL)\"\nso that a test author can transcribe it into a Rust test without re-reading the C. Use the pattern/subject/args explicitly.\nIf a rejection is UNREACHABLE in this build (JIT-only, 16/32-bit-only, EBCDIC, internal-corruption-only, needs >2GB allocation), write `UNREACHABLE` in the RECIPE field and explain why in <notes>.\n\nSort rows grouped by function. Aim for completeness over brevity — expect 120-200 rows.\n\nVERIFY: write `_v/checkrt.c`, a C program that implements each reachable row's recipe against c_src/build/libpcre2.so (compile with -I c_src/include -DPCRE2_CODE_UNIT_WIDTH=8) and prints \"<function> <expected> <actual> ok|MISMATCH\". You may hard-code the recipes in C in the same order as the TSV rows. Iterate until there are 0 MISMATCHes: when a row mismatches, the RECIPE is wrong — fix the recipe in the TSV (never blame the C library). Leave _v/checkrt.c in place and make sure it compiles and runs cleanly.\n\nFINAL REPORT: number of rows, number verified by running checkrt, number UNREACHABLE, and any rejection you could not reach with a recipe."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"ca