{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_n2y6rjfknhp6xzp723yfeejp3llbwzcykwxxn5k53b2ya5tvgaaa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_015QwkpUyFvJ7XA1rUHAaQD3","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Tests for DFA and next_match rows","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":true,"prompt":"Read /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wwiWoN/_v/AGENT_BRIEF.md FIRST and follow it exactly.\n\nYour assignment: rows **C158–C169** of `_v/configs.tsv` — `pcre2_dfa_match_8` (workspace size,\n`PCRE2_DFA_RESTART`, `PCRE2_DFA_SHORTEST`, the multi-match ovector semantics, the\n`DFA_UITEM`/`UCOND`/`UFUNC`/`UINVALID_UTF`/`DFA_RECURSE` unsupported-item paths, DFA limits,\npartial matching) plus `pcre2_next_match_8` and the bumpalong logic (C168–C169).\n\nWrite ONE file: `translation/tests/configs_dfa.rs`.\n\nGuidance specific to your rows:\n* Compile in EACH library separately, match with that library's own code object, and compare\n  with `read_match(api, md, rc)` (rc + full ovector + startchar + mark) plus, for DFA, the\n  ENTIRE ovector as filled by the multi-match logic (DFA fills ovector pairs with all\n  match lengths at one start point — compare all of them, and the `rc` when the ovector is\n  too small).\n* `pcre2_dfa_match_8` takes a workspace: `wscount` in `int`s. Sweep small workspace sizes\n  (20 is the documented minimum; try 0, 1, 19, 20, 21, 100, 1000, 20000) — the C returns\n  `PCRE2_ERROR_DFA_WSSIZE` (-43) when it is too small for the pattern, and the exact point at\n  which it starts failing is a strong differential check: for each of a set of patterns of\n  increasing complexity, find the smallest workspace for which the C succeeds and assert the\n  Rust has exactly the same threshold. Also assert the workspace CONTENTS the two libraries\n  leave behind are identical after a `PCRE2_PARTIAL_SOFT` match that is later continued with\n  `PCRE2_DFA_RESTART` (that is what restart reads back).\n* `PCRE2_DFA_RESTART`: run a partial match, then restart with the same workspace over the\n  continued subject; compare the rc/ovector of both stages, for both libraries. Include the\n  case where the restart data is from a *different* pattern (the C detects some of these).\n* Unsupported items: find what the DFA rejects by grepping\n  `grep -n \"PCRE2_ERROR_DFA_UITEM\\|DFA_UCOND\\|DFA_UFUNC\\|DFA_RECURSE\" c_src/src/pcre2_dfa_match.c`\n  and build a pattern for EACH distinct site (e.g. `\\C` in UTF, backreferences,\n  `(?(R)...)` recursion conditions, `(*ACCEPT)` inside assertions, callouts returning\n  unsupported values, `(?=...)`-internal recursion). Assert identical error codes.\n* Randomize hard with a fixed seed: a pool of DFA-compatible patterns × random subjects\n  (length 0..40, alphabets: ASCII, high bytes, valid UTF-8 2/3/4-byte sequences, invalid\n  UTF-8, newline shapes LF/CR/CRLF/NEL/U+2028/U+2029, NUL bytes) × start_offset 0..=len ×\n  option combinations (ANCHORED, ENDANCHORED, NOTBOL, NOTEOL, NOTEMPTY, NOTEMPTY_ATSTART,\n  PARTIAL_SOFT, PARTIAL_HARD, DFA_SHORTEST, NO_UTF_CHECK, and the UTF/UCP/MULTILINE/DOTALL\n  compile options) × several newline conventions and BSR settings. Aim for >= 100 000\n  comparisons while keeping the file under ~10 minutes.\n* Also compare `pcre2_dfa_match_8` against `pcre2_match_8` WITHIN each library only as a\n  sanity check if you like, but the assertion that matters is C-vs-Rust.\n* C168–C169 `pcre2_next_match_8`: read its C source\n  (`c_src/src/pcre2_match_next.c`) to learn the exact contract (it takes the match data plus\n  `*offset` and `*options` in/out). Drive a full iteration loop over a subject with\n  overlapping/empty matches (patterns like `a*`, `(?=a)`, `\\b`, `x?`) and compare the entire\n  SEQUENCE of (rc, offset, options, ovector) tuples produced by the two libraries until the\n  loop terminates, for many patterns and subjects, including empty subjects, subjects that are\n  all newlines, CRLF newline conventions (where the bumpalong must skip 2 units), and UTF\n  subjects (where it must skip whole characters).\n\nCover EVERY id C158–C169. Then report per the brief."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6708,"cache_read_input_tokens":137448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6708,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4f10895-a33e-435f-9410-97f386d0c503","uuid":"ed74d157-fdc1-42dc-8ccc-8554f4084048","timestamp":"2026-09-08T19:21:20.169Z"}
