{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_012g1BRrq8A6UZfCxiocpiQJ","type":"tool_result","content":"C195\tpcre2_pattern_convert\tPCRE2_CONVERT_POSIX_BASIC; also with PCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB_NO_WILD_SEPARATOR and PCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB_NO_STARSTAR set (silently ignored)\tinputs exercising every basic-mode branch: \"\", \"a\", \"a*b\", \"*a\", \"(a\", \"\\\\(a\\\\)\", \"\\\\{2\\\\}\", \"\\\\1\", \"\\\\z\", \"^a\", \"a^b\", \"(^a\", \"$a\", \"a$\", \"[abc]\", \"[^abc]\", \"[]abc]\", \"[a-z]\", \"[[:alpha:]]\", \"[[:alpha:\", \"[abc\", \"a\\\\\", \"**\", \"(*\", \"?a\", \"+a\", \"{2}\", \"|a\", \".a\", and a multi-byte UTF character\tthe basic converter treats \\\\( \\\\) \\\\{ \\\\} \\\\1-\\\\9 as metacharacters, escapes bare ( ) ? + { } |, has a leading-* rule and a leading-^ rule, and has its own POSIX-class state machine (pcre2_convert.c:149-384)\nC196\tpcre2_pattern_convert\tPCRE2_CONVERT_POSIX_EXTENDED\tthe same input list as C195 plus \"(a)\", \"(a\", \")a\", \"a|b\", \"a?\", \"a+\", \"a{2,3}\", \"a\\\\1\", \"\\\\(\", \"^a^b\", \"***\"\tthe extended converter treats ( ) ? + { } | as metacharacters directly, decrements a bracket counter on ), always makes ^ special, and takes the other branch of every escape and star rule (pcre2_convert.c:302-360)\nC197\tpcre2_pattern_convert\tPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB, with glob_separator '/', '\\\\' and '.' and glob_escape 0, '\\\\', '`' and '*'\tstar and question inputs: \"\", \"a\", \"*\", \"**\", \"***\", \"?\", \"a?b\", \"*a\", \"a*\", \"a**b\", \"**/x\", \"/**\", \"a/**/b\", \"a/**\\\\/b\", \"**\" at the end, \"*\" at the end, \"\\\\*\", a trailing escape character\tthe glob converter has distinct branches for a leading *, a leading **, ** after a separator, ** at the end (no_slash_z), a single * at the end (possessive quantifier), the atomic-group prefix after **, and the (*COMMIT) prefix otherwise; a trailing escape gives PCRE2_ERROR_CONVERT_SYNTAX (pcre2_convert.c:891-1060)\nC198\tpcre2_pattern_convert\tPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB with each separator and escape combination\tclass inputs: \"[abc]\", \"[!abc]\", \"[^abc]\", \"[]abc]\", \"[a-z]\", \"[z-a]\" (CONVERT_SYNTAX), \"[9-0]\" (CONVERT_SYNTAX), \"[a-[:digit:]]\" (CONVERT_SYNTAX), \"[[:digit:]]\", \"[[:punct:]]\", \"[[:graph:]]\", \"[[:print:]]\", \"[[:ascii:]]\", \"[[:nosuch:]]\" (falls through to literal handling), \"[a\", \"[!\", \"[\", \"[-a]\", \"[a-]\", \"[/]\", \"[.]\", \"[\\\\\\\\]\", \"[a\\\\]b]\", each of the 14 POSIX class names, and a class whose range spans the separator\tthe class parser has separate branches for negation, a leading ], POSIX classes, ranges (including the two CONVERT_SYNTAX cases), escaped members, and the separator_seen flag that appends a negative lookbehind on the closing bracket (pcre2_convert.c:636-804)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_017yDT6CP8kKVxB7m3jf7CCW","session_id":"a4f10895-a33e-435f-9410-97f386d0c503","uuid":"f3975763-ae54-41d1-9951-6e6ff935e86c","timestamp":"2026-09-08T19:22:26.955Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Tests for study/autopossess/context rows"}
