ow\n  (`row <n> <function> <expected>`) so `--nocapture` shows the mapping. Assert per row with a\n  message naming the row number, function, c_file:line and the trigger, so a failure\n  identifies the row immediately.\n* Add a final `#[test]` that asserts the row accounting: total rows parsed == 300, reachable\n  == 267, and that the number of rows you actually executed == 267. That guarantees no row is\n  silently dropped. Keep a per-function list of executed row numbers to make this exact.\n* Where a recipe needs a custom allocator (heap-limit / NOMEMORY rows), write the allocator in\n  Rust and install it via `pcre2_general_context_create_8`; anything allocated by a library\n  must be freed by that same library.\n* Where a recipe needs an out-of-range enum value across the FFI boundary (e.g. an unknown\n  `pcre2_set_optimize_8` directive, an unknown `PCRE2_CONFIG_*` key, an unknown\n  `PCRE2_INFO_*` key, an undefined option bit, an invalid newline convention), pass the raw\n  integer — C enums accept any int, so these are real inputs the Rust must handle identically.\n* In ADDITION to the table rows, add generic-boundary tests (still C-vs-Rust): NULL pointers\n  for every pointer argument of every public function that documents a NULL check; zero\n  lengths; `PCRE2_ZERO_TERMINATED` lengths; oversized lengths; `start_offset == length` and\n  `length + 1`; ovector count 0; and one value one step past each documented valid range.\n* The 33 UNREACHABLE rows must be counted and printed as skipped with their reason from the\n  notes field — do not attempt to trigger them.\n\nCover EVERY reachable row. Then report per the brief, including the row numbers of any row\nwhose C result did not match its tabulated expectation (that would mean the table is stale —\nreport it, do not silently adjust the expectation)."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6708,"cache_read_input_tokens":137448,"cac