{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"abcb389fec0b48571","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_018iFZtXDAnceYbcA4LYbw1G","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-zvTOcT/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-wwiWoN/a4f10895-a33e-435f-9410-97f386d0c503/tasks/abcb389fec0b48571.output","summary":"## 1. File written and rows covered\n\n**`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-wwiWoN/translation/tests/configs_match.rs`** (2975 lines) — one `#[test]` per row, every id **C126–C157** covered (32 tests, 32 row ids):\n\n`c126_null_length_and_option_checks`, `c127_start_offset`, `c128_notbol_noteol`, `c129_notempty`, `c130_anchored_endanchored`, `c131_offset_limit`, `c132_start_optimizations`, `c133_firstline`, `c134_match_limit`, `c135_depth_limit`, `c136_heap_limit`, `c137_recurse_loop_check`, `c138_copy_matched_subject`, `c139_no_jit`, `c140_utf_check`, `c141_match_invalid_utf`, `c142_partial_matching`, `c143_newline_conventions`, `c144_backreferences`, `c145_recursion`, `c146_atomic_and_possessive`, `c147_assertions`, `c148_scan_substring`, `c149_backslash_k`, `c150_verbs`, `c151_script_runs`, `c152_extuni`, `c153_classes`, `c154_callouts`, `c155_anchors_and_boundaries`, `c156_any_and_anybyte`, `c157_ovector_sizes`.\n\nMechanics: a `Duo` compiles each pattern separately in each `.so` (objects never mixed), creates the match data with the same library, primes it (a fresh match-data block leaves `mark`/`startchar` uninitialised — pcre2_match_data.c:56-70 — so early-error returns would otherwise compare malloc garbage), pre-fills both ovectors with a sentinel so unwritten slots compare deterministically, then compares `read_match` (rc + full ovector + startchar + `(*MARK)`), `pcre2_get_ovector_count`, `pcre2_get_match_data_size`, and `pcre2_get_match_data_heapframes_size_8` on the limit rows (C134-C137, C145). C154 mirrors the LP64 `pcre2_callout_block` (offsets derived from `c_src/include/pcre2.h:581-602`), reads every field with `read_unaligned` into a thread-local log per library and compares every field of every invocation, plus asserts version==2, ovector slots 0/1 == PCRE2_UNSET, the `subject`/`callout_data` pointers, and callout returns 0/1/-1/-1000/99. C138 overwrites the original subject buffer after the match and re-reads `pcre2_substring_get_bynumber_8`. C134 sweeps `match_limit` contiguously over 0..=3000; C135 over 0..=220 plus coarse values and `pcre2_set_recursion_limit`; C136 contiguously 0..=300 plus the frame-size boundary values; C131 sweeps the offset limit over 0..=len+1 and `PCRE2_UNSET`.\n\n## 2. Command and results\n\n```\ncd translation && CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$TMPDIR/tgt-final timeout 890 \\\n  cargo test --offline --test configs_match -- --nocapture\n```\n**32 passed, 0 failed**, 1.8 s wall (≈15 s CPU). **19,662,129 match comparisons** total (per-row counts printed with `--nocapture`; largest: C156 2.95M, C153 2.02M, C143 2.05M, C141 1.39M, C155 1.30M).\n\nMutation-checked the harness: temporarily XOR-ing `PCRE2_NOTBOL` into only the Rust call makes 26 of the 32 tests report a DIVERGENCE, so the comparisons are live (the 6 that don't are rows where NOTBOL is irrelevant).\n\n## 3. Divergences\n\n**No divergences** in any compared output (rc, full ovector, startchar, mark, ovector count, match-data size, heapframes size, callout-block fields, substring bytes) across all 19.7M comparisons.\n\nOne **robustness difference** found while building the tests, on input that PCRE2 documents as *undefined behaviour* (`PCRE2_NO_UTF_CHECK` with an invalid-UTF subject), so it is not a spec divergence but is worth recording:\n\n- Row C152 / C140. Pattern `b\"\\\\X\"`, compile options `PCRE2_UTF`, subject `b\"a\\xf8\\x88\\x80\\x80\\x80\"`, `start_offset 0`, match options `PCRE2_NO_UTF_CHECK`.\n  - **C**: `rc = 1`.\n  - **Rust**: `panicked at src/extuni.rs:47: index out of bounds: the len is 8704 but the index is 16384` → the whole process aborts (SIGABRT).\n  - Computed by `UCD_GRAPHBREAK(c)` → `GET_UCD(c)` → `REAL_GET_UCD` = `PRIV(ucd_stage1)[c/UCD_BLOCK_SIZE]` (`c_src/src/pcre2_internal.h:2123-2131,2145`), reached from `c_src/src/pcre2_extuni.c:104`. In the 8-bit build `GET_UCD` has **no** `ch > MAX_UTF_CODE_POINT` guard (that arm is 32-bit only, pcre2_internal.h:2128), and `GETCHARLEN` on a 5/6-byte lead byte yields `c > 0x10FFFF`; `ucd_stage1` has only `0x110000/128 = 8704` entries. The C silently reads adjacent static data; the Rust slice index is bounds-checked and panics. No fix attempted.\n\n## 4. Not tested, and why\n\n- **`PCRE2_NO_UTF_CHECK` combined with an invalid-UTF subject and a plain-`PCRE2_UTF` pattern** is skipped by the `ub_combo()` guard in the file. Reason: documented UB, and as shown above the C itself indexes outside `PRIV(ucd_stage1)`, so there is no defined C behaviour to differentiate against. Every *other* invalid-UTF combination **is** tested: invalid subjects with the check on (C140/C141), and invalid subjects with `PCRE2_NO_UTF_CHECK` **plus** `PCRE2_MATCH_INVALID_UTF` (which forces the check anyway, pcre2_match.c:7274) so the whole fragment machinery of C141 is exercised with NO_UTF_CHECK set.\n- **C138's \"a general context whose malloc fails\"**: not tested. Injecting a failing allocator would require handing one library's allocator callbacks a state shared with the other, and the resulting `PCRE2_ERROR_NOMEMORY` path is allocator-timing dependent, so it is not a byte-deterministic differential comparison. Everything else in the row (length 0 → NULL copied pointer, non-empty subject, reuse after a successful copy, failing match with the option set, and the `PCRE2_MD_COPIED_SUBJECT`-driven substring behaviour after the original buffer is trashed) is covered.\n- **C136's \"60000 capture groups\"**: used 5000 groups instead (`\"()\"*5000`), because 60000 groups is a 120 000-code-unit pattern and `MAX_PATTERN_SIZE` is `1<<16`. 5000 groups already makes `frame_size*10` exceed `1024*heap_limit` for the small limits, i.e. it exercises the \"even one frame does not fit → immediate `PCRE2_ERROR_HEAPLIMIT`\" branch.\n- Three patterns in the pools legitimately fail to compile in **both** libraries and are reported as `NOTE ... case skipped` (compile error codes are asserted equal first): `[\\x{100}-\\x{200}]` without `PCRE2_UTF` (ERR134). The other two originally-failing patterns were replaced with compiling equivalents (`(?:(?C1)a)*b`, `(?C{x}}y})a`, `(?i)(i)\\1` with `PCRE2_UTF`+`EXTRA_TURKISH_CASING`, which needs UTF — ERR204 otherwise).\n\nNo files other than `translation/tests/configs_match.rs` were created or modified.","usage":{"total_tokens":219585,"tool_uses":63,"duration_ms":1434916},"uuid":"7088251a-cb57-478c-8e12-74af260d316a","session_id":"a4f10895-a33e-435f-9410-97f386d0c503"}
